'' ON ERROR GOTO plants a setjmp, and a throw from ANOTHER procedure lands in it.
''
'' fbc's error path is a cross-function computed goto: the thrower returns the
'' handler's address and the raising procedure does 'goto *vr$1'. GCC takes that;
'' LLVM's indirectbr cannot leave the function it appears in, so tiko could only
'' do it when the throw and its ON ERROR were in the SAME procedure. Anywhere
'' else it emitted 'unreachable', which LLVM lowers to int3 -- measured as
'' STATUS_BREAKPOINT on com/comptr-error-path.bas and on a four-line repro.
''
'' The frame is captured where the handler is installed, because that is the
'' frame being jumped into, and lib/rt/src/c/errjmp.c longjmps from wherever the
'' throw happened. This asserts the four instructions that make that work; the
'' src file records what the VALUES do, and why they are not asserted.
''
'' THE SPELLING OF setjmp FOLLOWS THE C LIBRARY, not the platform: win64-msvc
'' links ucrtbase, which exports '__intrinsic_setjmp' and not '_setjmp', and
'' win64 links MinGW's msvcrt, which is the other way round. '_setjmp' is a
'' substring of both, so one assertion covers every target.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/on-error-setjmp.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' The handler's address is still installed, as fbc does it -- the runtime hands
'' it back and the jump block branches through it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_ErrorSetHandler(blockaddr

'' The landing point: a buffer from the runtime, setjmp into it, and the arm that
'' runs when longjmp arrives asks which handler is current and jumps there.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_ErrorJmpBuf()
'' EXPECT-OUT: _setjmp(
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_ErrorGetHandler()
'' EXPECT-OUT: indirectbr

'' AND THE THROW ITSELF STAYS UNREACHABLE-TERMINATED. It is correct now rather
'' than a hazard: with a buffer armed the runtime does not return, and with none
'' it aborts, so neither path falls through.
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_ErrorThrowEx
